.TH 9SHARE 8 "2026" "9share" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
9share \- share directories between machines over 9P, through ssh
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B 9share export
.I DIR HOST
.RI [ NAME ]
.br
.B 9share unexport
.I NAME
.br
.B sudo 9share mount
.I USER NAME DIR
.br
.B sudo 9share unmount
.I NAME
.br
.B 9share
.BR rw | ro | off
.I NAME
.br
.B 9share status
.br
.B sudo 9share wsl\-boot
.br
.B 9share boot
.RB [ \-\-detach ]
.br
.B 9share serve
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B 9share
makes a directory on one machine appear on another, with the kernel's 9p client, through an ssh tunnel that the machine with the
files opens.  The machine that shows the files needs no open port.  Install the same package on both machines; a machine is an
exporter, a receiver or both, according to what is configured on it, and does nothing until something is.
.PP
The server is
.BR daud (8),
a fork of diod, run as the exporting user.
.SS On the machine that has the files (as an ordinary user)
.TP
.BI "export " "DIR HOST " [ NAME ]
Share DIR with HOST (a name as ssh knows it) as NAME, by default the directory's own name.  The block is added to
.IR ~/.config/9share/9share.conf ;
the running service notices within a few seconds.  Prints the command to run on HOST.
.TP
.BI unexport " NAME"
Stop sharing it.
.SS On the machine that shows them
.TP
.BI "mount " "USER NAME DIR"
(root) Show the share NAME that arrives in the account USER of this machine (the account the exporter logs in as) at DIR.  The block
is added to
.IR /etc/9share/9share.conf .
It is mounted while the exporter's tunnel is up and unmounted while it is not.
.TP
.BI unmount " NAME"
(root) Remove it and unmount.
.TP
.BR rw | ro | off " \fINAME\fP"
(as USER, no sudo) Mounted read-write (the default), read-only, or not mounted.  Follows within a few seconds.
.SS On either
.TP
.B status
What is shared, what is mounted, and whether the daemon runs.
.TP
.B wsl\-boot
(root, WSL2) Put the daemon in the [boot] command of
.IR /etc/wsl.conf ,
which WSL2 has instead of systemd.  A command already there is kept and runs first, from
.IR /etc/9share/wsl\-boot .
.SS Behind these
.TP
.B boot
The root daemon, run by the
.B 9share
service (or with
.BR \-\-detach ,
in the background, logging to /var/log/9share.log).  Every two seconds it starts
.B serve
for each user who has a configuration file and no
.B serve
running, and makes the mounts of the system configuration agree with the tunnels and the users' modes.  A mount whose tunnel has
died is unmounted lazily and mounted again when the tunnel is back.
.TP
.B serve
One user's exports, in the foreground.  For each export it runs
.B daud
(as that user, on a private unix socket in
.IR ~/.local/state/9share/NAME/ )
and
.BR "ssh \-R" ,
which puts that socket at
.I ~/.9share/NAME.sock
on HOST.  Each is started again when it dies, after 1, 2, 4 ... 30 seconds; ssh runs with ServerAliveInterval 15 and
ServerAliveCountMax 3, so a dead link is noticed in about 45 seconds.  Before it starts ssh it prepares HOST: it makes
.IR ~/.9share ,
removes the stale socket a dead tunnel left, and writes
.I ~/.9share/NAME.info
with the user name and directory that the mount asks daud for.  The configuration file is read again whenever it changes, and only
the exports whose block changed are restarted.
.SH SECURITY
Mounts are made nosuid,nodev, with access=any: any user on the receiving machine who can reach the mount point sees the files with
the exporting user's rights.  The mount is only made from a socket that belongs to USER and that answers, and every value that goes
from the exporter's files into a mount option is checked first.  Do not export a directory to a machine whose users you do not trust.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NINESHARE_SYSCONFDIR
Where the system configuration is looked for (default: the sysconfdir given to configure, plus /9share).  For tests.
.TP
.B NINESHARE_DAUD
The daud program to run (default: the one installed with 9share).  For tests.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/9share/9share.conf
.TQ
.I /etc/9share/9share.conf
The exports of a user and the mounts of a machine.  See
.BR 9share.conf (5).
.TP
.I ~/.config/9share/mode.d/NAME
The mode (rw, ro, off) of a mount, written by
.BR "9share rw|ro|off" .
.TP
.I ~/.9share/
On the receiving machine: the socket and the .info file of each share.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR 9share.conf (5),
.BR daud (8),
.BR ssh (1),
.BR mount (8)
